feat: frame the crop by hand, and pick pictures from the gallery

The cropper kept its window fixed and slid the picture behind it, so the user
could neither see what was being cut away nor select a rectangle the app had not
already offered. The picture is now fitted whole onto the screen with the frame
drawn on top of it: drag a corner to size the frame, drag inside it to move it,
drag outside it to draw a new one, pinch to look closer. A free shape joins the
fixed ratios, the size of the kept part is announced under the picture, and the
file that comes out is exactly the frame.

The handle hit test also compared against the anchor corner rather than the grip
itself, which made every corner drag resize the frame from the opposite corner:
the frame never ended up where the finger went. Both that fix and the
interaction are covered by a new page test that drives real drags.

Pictures are chosen from the device's own gallery now: Android 13's photo picker
first (one screen of thumbnails, no storage permission), its Play services
backport next, the gallery app's own picker after that, and the document picker
only on a device with no gallery at all. Videos, documents and recordings still
go through the document picker.
